tools.cdt.evaluate_script({ pageId: 1, function: () => window.location.href })
and got "Specify exactly one of function or sourcePath."

That's odd — it says specify exactly one of them, meaning it might have detected both as set or neither. Possibly the harness converts the arrow function `() => window.location.href` and something. Let me instead pass function as a STRING: `"() => window.location.href"`. That's the documented approach ("function?: string"). Yes! The signature shows `function?: string`. I passed a JS function object; it may not serialize correctly, causing both to appear unset or ambiguous. Let me pass strings.

So for extraction, I'll pass function as a string of the arrow function body. Let me build a comprehensive extraction as a string.

Let me write the extraction script carefully as a string. eBay SRP modern selectors:
- Container: `div.srp-results-core` containing `ul` of items? Actually each listing item is `<li class="listItem">` or `<div class="listItem">`. Modern eBay uses `<ul>` maybe. Let me query all elements with class `listItem` under `.srp-results-core`, but also there could be gallery side cards (class `results collagecardcontainer` / `listRefinements`). To get main list, target `div.srp-results` > div list items. 

A robust approach used by many: `document.querySelectorAll('.srp-results .srp-results-core div.listItem')` — but the new UI puts titles and price in specific attrs. Let me extract using data attributes that eBay sets:
- title attr: on `<a class="s-item-link">` there is `title` attribute with full text (e.g., "Sony WH-1000XM6 Wireless Noise Cancelling Headphones ... 50% Off..."). That includes price suffix sometimes. Hmm.
- Better to parse child elements.

Given complexity, let me capture raw fields from each `.listItem`:
```
() => {
  const items = [...document.querySelectorAll('div.srp-results-core > div.listItem')];
  const grab = (el, sel) => { const e = el.querySelector(sel); return e ? e.textContent.trim() : ''; };
  const out = [];
  for (const li of items) {
    // main link
    const a = li.querySelector('a.s-item-link') || li.querySelector('.s-item-title');
    const link = (li.querySelector('a.s-item-link')) ? (li.querySelector('a.s-item-link').href||'') : '';
